<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Fabricator.Bridge.Tests</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Runs on both TFMs (inherited): net8.0 is the deployment floor, net10.0 the default. -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="Xunit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ⚠ THERE IS NO ProjectReference TO Fabricator.Bridge, AND ADDING ONE WOULD BREAK THIS TIER.

    Referencing it here would make these tests require a large restore — Apache.Arrow, the Fabric SDK and
    the Azure SDKs — and tier 0's whole point is that it runs with no C++, no vcpkg and no submodules, in
    about two minutes.

    ⚠ ONE HALF OF THE ORIGINAL REASON IS GONE, so do not quote this as if it still had two. Until
    2026-08-18 this said Bridge project-references "engineered-wood (a git SUBMODULE)" as well, which was
    the harder blocker: a submodule is not restorable, it has to be initialised. The assembly split moved
    the Delta provider — and with it the engineered-wood ProjectReference, AWSSDK.S3 and SqlClient — into
    Fabricator.Delta, so BRIDGE IS NOW SUBMODULE-FREE. What still stands is the restore cost, which is a
    speed argument rather than a structural one; a future pass could weigh it on its own merits.

    So this project COMPILES SELECTED BRIDGE SOURCE FILES directly. The admission rule is the
    constraint that keeps the tier cheap:

        a Bridge file belongs here only if its closure is the .NET base class library.

    That is a feature, not a workaround. It rewards keeping decision logic — parsing, resolution,
    rendering — free of the Arrow/SDK boundary, which is exactly what makes it testable. A file that
    cannot be admitted is telling you it has business logic entangled with I/O.

    To test something new: if it is already BCL-only, link it below. If it is not, extract the
    decidable part into a file that is (FabricVariableLibraryFormat.cs was split out of
    FabricVariableFunctions.cs for precisely this reason), and link that.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="..\Fabricator.Bridge\FabricApi\FabricVariableLibraryFormat.cs"
             Link="Linked\FabricVariableLibraryFormat.cs" />
    <!--
      The Fabric SQL endpoint host/connstr derivation. Admissible because its closure is the BCL (it hand-rolls
      both the base32 decode — the BCL has none — and the connection-string parse, rather than reaching for
      SqlConnectionStringBuilder, precisely so it can be tested here). It is also the code that most NEEDS
      offline tests: it decodes an UNDOCUMENTED host encoding observed on one tenant, and its required
      behaviour on anything unexpected is to return null rather than a plausible-looking wrong workspace id.
    -->
    <Compile Include="..\Fabricator.Bridge\FabricApi\FabricSqlEndpointHost.cs"
             Link="Linked\FabricSqlEndpointHost.cs" />
    <!--
      The persisted Delta parquet tuning (the fabricator.parquet.* table properties). Admissible because the
      compression name and parquet version stay STRINGS in it — their enums live on the engine side — which is
      what keeps its closure the BCL. Worth testing here rather than only through SQL: the round trip is what
      guarantees a property written by a CREATE is read back as the same tuning, and the malformed-value cases
      can be reached from SQL only via delta.set_tblproperties, so each would otherwise cost a
      service-tier round trip to cover.
    -->
    <Compile Include="..\Fabricator.Bridge\DeltaParquetProperties.cs"
             Link="Linked\DeltaParquetProperties.cs" />
    <!--
      The Fabric Warehouse COPY INTO staging root. Admissible because it is pure string derivation. It is the
      third place in this codebase where one storage account must be named differently by us and by the SQL
      engine, and the first two both shipped a bug in that translation — so the round trip is worth pinning
      offline rather than only through a live Fabric load.
    -->
    <Compile Include="..\Fabricator.Bridge\OneLakeStagingLocation.cs"
             Link="Linked\OneLakeStagingLocation.cs" />
    <!--
      The provider-settings store. Admissible because its closure is the BCL (ConcurrentDictionary +
      AsyncLocal). Worth testing here rather than only through SQL: the session layer's edges — session 0
      meaning GLOBAL, a null session value shadowing as UNSET, ClearSession being idempotent for a session
      that never set anything — are each one line to get wrong and invisible from SQL until a dbt run leaks
      one model's setting into another's.
    -->
    <Compile Include="..\Fabricator.Abstractions\ProviderSettings.cs"
             Link="Linked\ProviderSettings.cs" />
    <!--
      The Fabric token-service URL composition. Admissible because it is pure string slicing (it exists
      SPLIT OUT of FabricNotebookCredential, which pulls in Azure.Core, precisely so it can be tested here).
      Worth pinning offline for the same reason as FabricSqlEndpointHost: the shape is UNDOCUMENTED, read
      off a live runtime, and the one part that looks like a formatting detail — the DOUBLED slash in
      .../automatic//token — is what the service 404s without. A live Fabric run costs minutes and a Spark
      session; this costs milliseconds and catches the case a URI parser would normalise away.
    -->
    <Compile Include="..\Fabricator.Bridge\FabricTokenServiceUrl.cs"
             Link="Linked\FabricTokenServiceUrl.cs" />
    <!--
      The table_alter request doc (ABI v74). Admissible because the spec is a plain record over
      System.Text.Json — the new column's TYPE deliberately does not live in it (it rides beside the doc as
      an Arrow field, since a VARIANT is identified by field metadata), which is exactly what keeps the
      closure the BCL. Worth pinning offline: this is the one ABI doc the HOST writes and the bridge reads,
      so nothing but a full build exercises the pair, and the states that matter are the ones a wrong parse
      makes silent — set_default's JSON null (DEFAULT NULL) versus an absent key, and an EMPTY `columns`
      array, which is the RESET spelling and must not read as "no list".
    -->
    <Compile Include="..\Fabricator.Abstractions\AlterTableSpec.cs"
             Link="Linked\AlterTableSpec.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
